    "lawCode": "Amd §305, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6581\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the education law, in relation to\ndirecting the commissioner of education to issue a guidance memorandum\nto every school district and board of cooperative educational services\nto inform them of the unique educational needs of students with\ndyslexia, dyscalculia and dysgraphia to issue a guidance memorandum to\nevery school district and board of cooperative educational services to\ninform them of the unique educational needs of students with dyslexia,\ndyscalculia and dysgraphia\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to inform school districts about the\nunique educational needs of students with dyslexia and other related\ndisorders.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends Education Law section 305 to require the Commissioner\nof Education to issue a guidance memorandum to every school district\nand BOCES to inform them of the unique educational needs of students\nwith dyslexia and other related disorders and to clarify that school\ndistricts may reference or use the terms dyslexia, dyscalculia and\ndysgraphia in evaluations, eligibility determinations or in developing\nan IEP.\n\nSection 2 provides for the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe federal Individuals with Disabilities Act (IDEA) classifies\ndyslexia as a specific learning disability, however, school districts\nhave been reluctant to reference dyslexia and other related disorders\nwhen developing Individualized Education Plans (IEPs). In October\n2015, the U.S. Department of Education, issued guidance to \"clarify\nthat there is nothing in the IDEA that would prohibit the use of the\nterms dyslexia, dyscalculia and dysgraphia in IDEA evaluation,\neligibility determinations, or IEP documents.\" This bill would help\ninform school districts and BOCES about the educational needs of\nstudents with dyslexia, so that early interventions and services can\nbe provided to help these children become proficient readers.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nUnknown.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
